{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014dCNUYYUgdgiGWgwizprWa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"012_moe_expert_batched_execution_with_capacity_factor\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Optimized batched MoE expert execution with capacity-based token batching. Groups tokens by assigned experts using capacity factors, executes all experts in parallel using batched matrix operations, and performs efficient scatter-gather for weighted aggregation. Uses SwiGLU activation (silu(gate) * up) for expert computation. Capacity factor is fixed at 1.25.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"num_tokens\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Total number of tokens (batch_size * seq_len)\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"hidden_size\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n12\t            \"value\": 6144,\n13\t            \"description\": \"Input/output hidden dimension\"\n14\t        },\n15\t        \"moe_intermediate_size\": {\n16\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n17\t            \"value\": 2560,\n18\t            \"description\": \"Expert intermediate dimension\"\n19\t        },\n20\t        \"num_experts\": {\n21\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n22\t            \"value\": 160,\n23\t            \"description\": \"Total number of experts\"\n24\t        },\n25\t        \"num_experts_per_tok\": {\n26\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n27\t            \"value\": 8,\n28\t            \"description\": \"Number of experts selected per token\"\n29\t        }\n30\t    },\n31\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n32\t    \"inputs\": {\n33\t        \"hidden_states\": {\n34\t            \"shape\": [\n35\t                \"num_tokens\",\n36\t                \"hidden_size\"\n37\t            ],\n38\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n39\t            \"description\": \"Input hidden states\"\n40\t        },\n41\t        \"selected_experts\": {\n42\t            \"shape\": [\n43\t                \"num_tokens\",\n44\t                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n45\t            ],\n46\t            \"dtype\": \"int64\",\n47\t            \"description\": \"Top-k expert indices for each token\"\n48\t        },\n49\t        \"routing_weights\": {\n50\t            \"shape\": [\n51\t                \"num_tokens\",\n52\t                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n53\t            ],\n54\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n55\t            \"description\": \"Routing weights for selected experts\"\n56\t        },\n57\t        \"expert_gate_weights\": {\n58\t            \"shape\": [\n59\t                \"num_experts\",\n60\t                \"hidden_size\",\n61\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n62\t            ],\n63\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n64\t            \"description\": \"Gate projection weights for all experts\"\n65\t        },\n66\t        \"expert_up_weights\": {\n67\t            \"shape\": [\n68\t                \"num_experts\",\n69\t                \"hidden_size\",\n70\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n71\t            ],\n72\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n73\t            \"description\": \"Up projection weights for all experts\"\n74\t        },\n75\t        \"expert_down_weights\": {\n76\t            \"shape\": [\n77\t                \"num_experts\",\n78\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n79\t                \"hidden_size\"\n80\t            ],\n81\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n82\t            \"description\": \"Down projection weights for all experts\"\n83\t        }\n84\t    },\n85\t    \"outputs\": {\n86\t        \"output\": {\n87\t            \"shape\": [\n88\t                \"num_tokens\",\n89\t                \"hidden_size\"\n90\t            ],\n91\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n92\t            \"description\": \"Aggregated expert outputs\"\n93\t        }\n94\t    },\n95\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\nimport math\\n\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict[str, torch.Tensor]:\\n    num_tokens = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_tokens\\\"]\\n    hidden_size = axes_and_scalars[\\\"hidden_size\\\"]\\n    moe_intermediate_size = axes_and_scalars[\\\"moe_intermediate_size\\\"]\\n    num_experts = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_experts\\\"]\\n    num_experts_per_tok = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_experts_per_tok\\\"]\\n\\n    dtype = torch.bfloat16\\n\\n    hidden_states = torch.randn(num_tokens, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n\\n    # Generate valid expert indices - each token selects num_experts_per_tok unique experts\\n    selected_experts = torch.zeros(num_tokens, num_experts_per_tok, dtype=torch.int64, device=device)\\n    for i in range(num_tokens):\\n        perm = torch.randperm(num_experts, device=device)[:num_experts_per_tok]\\n        selected_experts[i] = perm\\n\\n    # Generate routing weights that sum to 1 for each token\\n    routing_logits = torch.randn(num_tokens, num_experts_per_tok, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n    routing_weights = F.softmax(routing_logits.float(), dim=-1).to(dtype)\\n\\n    # Expert weights: Xavier init with 1/sqrt(fan_in)\\n    # gate/up: shape [num_experts, hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size], fan_in = hidden_size (dim 1)\\n    expert_gate_weights = torch.randn(num_experts, hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size, dtype=dtype, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size)\\n    expert_up_weights = torch.randn(num_experts, hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size, dtype=dtype, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size)\\n    # down: shape [num_experts, moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size], fan_in = moe_intermediate_size (dim 1)\\n    expert_down_weights = torch.randn(num_experts, moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device) / math.sqrt(moe_intermediate_size)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"hidden_states\\\": hidden_states,\\n        \\\"selected_experts\\\": selected_experts,\\n        \\\"routing_weights\\\": routing_weights,\\n        \\\"expert_gate_weights\\\": expert_gate_weights,\\n        \\\"expert_up_weights\\\": expert_up_weights,\\n        \\\"expert_down_weights\\\": expert_down_weights,\\n    }\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    selected_experts: torch.Tensor,\\n    routing_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    expert_gate_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    expert_up_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    expert_down_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    num_tokens, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape\\n    num_experts, _, moe_intermediate_size = expert_gate_weights.shape\\n    num_experts_per_tok = selected_experts.shape[1]\\n    device = hidden_states.device\\n    dtype = hidden_states.dtype\\n\\n    capacity = max(int((num_tokens * num_experts_per_tok / num_experts) * 1.25), 1)\\n\\n    # Flatten all token-expert assignments: (num_tokens * K,)\\n    flat_experts = selected_experts.reshape(-1)\\n    flat_weights = routing_weights.reshape(-1)\\n    flat_token_ids = torch.arange(num_tokens, device=device).repeat_interleave(num_experts_per_tok)\\n\\n    # Sort by expert ID (stable to match original sequential assignment order)\\n    sorted_experts, sorted_indices = flat_experts.sort(stable=True)\\n    sorted_weights = flat_weights[sorted_indices]\\n    sorted_token_ids = flat_token_ids[sorted_indices]\\n\\n    # OPT 1: Vectorized within-expert position computation.\\n    # After sorting, tokens for the same expert are contiguous.\\n    # Position within group = global_sorted_index - start_of_expert_group.\\n    counts = torch.bincount(sorted_experts, minlength=num_experts)\\n    starts = torch.zeros(num_experts, dtype=torch.long, device=device)\\n    starts[1:] = counts[:-1].cumsum(0)\\n\\n    within_pos = torch.arange(len(sorted_experts), device=device) - starts[sorted_experts]\\n\\n    # Apply capacity constraint (same semantics: first `capacity` per expert admitted)\\n    valid = within_pos < capacity\\n    v_exp = sorted_experts[valid]\\n    v_pos = within_pos[valid]\\n    v_tok = sorted_token_ids[valid]\\n    v_wt = sorted_weights[valid]\\n\\n    # Scatter tokens into padded expert batches using advanced indexing\\n    expert_inputs = torch.zeros(num_experts, capacity, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n    expert_inputs[v_exp, v_pos] = hidden_states[v_tok]\\n\\n    # Batched expert forward pass (same 3 BMMs as original)\\n    gate_out = torch.bmm(expert_inputs, expert_gate_weights)\\n    up_out = torch.bmm(expert_inputs, expert_up_weights)\\n\\n    # SwiGLU with in-place mul to save one allocation\\n    activated = F.silu(gate_out).mul_(up_out)\\n\\n    expert_outputs = torch.bmm(activated, expert_down_weights)\\n\\n    # OPT 2: Vectorized gather + weighted aggregation.\\n    # Gather only valid positions (skip mask \\u2014 invalid positions never read).\\n    valid_out = expert_outputs[v_exp, v_pos]  # (num_valid, hidden_size)\\n    weighted_out = v_wt.unsqueeze(1) * valid_out\\n\\n    result = torch.zeros(num_tokens, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n    result.index_add_(0, v_tok, weighted_out)\\n\\n    return result\\n\"\n96\t}\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84708b82-1793-4117-b276-8f509c6ef623","uuid":"3604d3b9-aa2f-4b5b-8280-b7cac5320446","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:54:37.025Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-012_moe_expert_batched_execution_with_capacity_factor/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"012_moe_expert_batched_execution_with_capacity_factor\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct\",\n    \"description\": \"Optimized batched MoE expert execution with capacity-based token batching. Groups tokens by assigned experts using capacity factors, executes all experts in parallel using batched matrix operations, and performs efficient scatter-gather for weighted aggregation. Uses SwiGLU activation (silu(gate) * up) for expert computation. Capacity factor is fixed at 1.25.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"num_tokens\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Total number of tokens (batch_size * seq_len)\"\n        },\n        \"hidden_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 6144,\n            \"description\": \"Input/output hidden dimension\"\n        },\n        \"moe_intermediate_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 2560,\n            \"description\": \"Expert intermediate dimension\"\n        },\n        \"num_experts\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 160,\n            \"description\": \"Total number of experts\"\n        },\n        \"num_experts_per_tok\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 8,\n            \"description\": \"Number of experts selected per token\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_tokens\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input hidden states\"\n        },\n        \"selected_experts\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_tokens\",\n                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"int64\",\n            \"description\": \"Top-k expert indices for each token\"\n        },\n        \"routing_weights\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_tokens\",\n                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Routing weights for selected experts\"\n        },\n        \"expert_gate_weights\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_experts\",\n                \"hidden_size\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Gate projection weights for all experts\"\n        },\n        \"expert_up_weights\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_experts\",\n                \"hidden_size\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Up projection weights for all experts\"\n        },\n        \"expert_down_weights\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_experts\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Down projection weights for all experts\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_tokens\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Aggregated expert outputs\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\nimport math\\n\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict[str, torch.Tensor]:\\n    num_tokens = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_tokens\\\"]\\n    hidden_size = axes_and_scalars[\\\"hidden_size\\\"]\\n    moe_intermediate_size = axes_and_scalars[\\\"moe_intermediate_size\\\"]\\n    num_experts = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_experts\\\"]\\n    num_experts_per_tok = axes_and_scalars[\\\"num_experts_per_tok\\\"]\\n\\n    dtype = torch.bfloat16\\n\\n    hidden_states = torch.randn(num_tokens, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n\\n    # Generate valid expert indices - each token selects num_experts_per_tok unique experts\\n    selected_experts = torch.zeros(num_tokens, num_experts_per_tok, dtype=torch.int64, device=device)\\n    for i in range(num_tokens):\\n        perm = torch.randperm(num_experts, device=device)[:num_experts_per_tok]\\n        selected_experts[i] = perm\\n\\n    # Generate routing weights that sum to 1 for each token\\n    routing_logits = torch.randn(num_tokens, num_experts_per_tok, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n    routing_weights = F.softmax(routing_logits.float(), dim=-1).to(dtype)\\n\\n    # Expert weights: Xavier init with 1/sqrt(fan_in)\\n    # gate/up: shape [num_experts, hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size], fan_in = hidden_size (dim 1)\\n    expert_gate_weights = torch.randn(num_experts, hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size, dtype=dtype, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size)\\n    expert_up_weights = torch.randn(num_experts, hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size, dtype=dtype, device=device) / math.sqrt(hidden_size)\\n    # down: shape [num_experts, moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size], fan_in = moe_intermediate_size (dim 1)\\n    expert_down_weights = torch.randn(num_experts, moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device) / math.sqrt(moe_intermediate_size)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"hidden_states\\\": hidden_states,\\n        \\\"selected_experts\\\": selected_experts,\\n        \\\"routing_weights\\\": routing_weights,\\n        \\\"expert_gate_weights\\\": expert_gate_weights,\\n        \\\"expert_up_weights\\\": expert_up_weights,\\n        \\\"expert_down_weights\\\": expert_down_weights,\\n    }\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    selected_experts: torch.Tensor,\\n    routing_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    expert_gate_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    expert_up_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    expert_down_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    num_tokens, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape\\n    num_experts, _, moe_intermediate_size = expert_gate_weights.shape\\n    num_experts_per_tok = selected_experts.shape[1]\\n    device = hidden_states.device\\n    dtype = hidden_states.dtype\\n\\n    capacity = max(int((num_tokens * num_experts_per_tok / num_experts) * 1.25), 1)\\n\\n    # Flatten all token-expert assignments: (num_tokens * K,)\\n    flat_experts = selected_experts.reshape(-1)\\n    flat_weights = routing_weights.reshape(-1)\\n    flat_token_ids = torch.arange(num_tokens, device=device).repeat_interleave(num_experts_per_tok)\\n\\n    # Sort by expert ID (stable to match original sequential assignment order)\\n    sorted_experts, sorted_indices = flat_experts.sort(stable=True)\\n    sorted_weights = flat_weights[sorted_indices]\\n    sorted_token_ids = flat_token_ids[sorted_indices]\\n\\n    # OPT 1: Vectorized within-expert position computation.\\n    # After sorting, tokens for the same expert are contiguous.\\n    # Position within group = global_sorted_index - start_of_expert_group.\\n    counts = torch.bincount(sorted_experts, minlength=num_experts)\\n    starts = torch.zeros(num_experts, dtype=torch.long, device=device)\\n    starts[1:] = counts[:-1].cumsum(0)\\n\\n    within_pos = torch.arange(len(sorted_experts), device=device) - starts[sorted_experts]\\n\\n    # Apply capacity constraint (same semantics: first `capacity` per expert admitted)\\n    valid = within_pos < capacity\\n    v_exp = sorted_experts[valid]\\n    v_pos = within_pos[valid]\\n    v_tok = sorted_token_ids[valid]\\n    v_wt = sorted_weights[valid]\\n\\n    # Scatter tokens into padded expert batches using advanced indexing\\n    expert_inputs = torch.zeros(num_experts, capacity, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n    expert_inputs[v_exp, v_pos] = hidden_states[v_tok]\\n\\n    # Batched expert forward pass (same 3 BMMs as original)\\n    gate_out = torch.bmm(expert_inputs, expert_gate_weights)\\n    up_out = torch.bmm(expert_inputs, expert_up_weights)\\n\\n    # SwiGLU with in-place mul to save one allocation\\n    activated = F.silu(gate_out).mul_(up_out)\\n\\n    expert_outputs = torch.bmm(activated, expert_down_weights)\\n\\n    # OPT 2: Vectorized gather + weighted aggregation.\\n    # Gather only valid positions (skip mask \\u2014 invalid positions never read).\\n    valid_out = expert_outputs[v_exp, v_pos]  # (num_valid, hidden_size)\\n    weighted_out = v_wt.unsqueeze(1) * valid_out\\n\\n    result = torch.zeros(num_tokens, hidden_size, dtype=dtype, device=device)\\n    result.index_add_(0, v_tok, weighted_out)\\n\\n    return result\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":97,"startLine":1,"totalLines":97}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K6W3WRpyapgjt2sDfYvcpB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/012 MoE Expert Batched Execution with Capacity Factor\n2\t\n3\tRun ID: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-012_moe_expert_batched_execution_with_capacity_factor`\n4\tTarget: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere). Primary implementation: **Triton**. PyTorch allowed only for\n5\ttensor metadata / launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n6\t\n7\tThis document is analysis only. No plan, no solution code is written yet (per workflow steps 2–4).\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. Operation semantics (from `task/definition.json`)\n12\t\n13\tCapacity-based, drop-if-overflow MoE feed-forward with SwiGLU experts.\n14\t\n15\tConstants (all fixed by the task axes):\n16\t\n17\t| symbol | meaning | value |\n18\t|---|---|---|\n19\t| `H` | hidden_size | 6144 |\n20\t| `I` | moe_intermediate_size | 2560 |\n21\t| `E` | num_experts | 160 |\n22\t| `K` | num_experts_per_tok | 8 |\n23\t| `cf` | capacity factor | 1.25 (fixed) |\n24\t| `dtype` | all tensors | bfloat16 (indices int64) |\n25\t\n26\tInputs:\n27\t- `hidden_states` `[N, H]` bf16\n28\t- `selected_experts` `[N, K]` int64 — each row is `K` **unique** expert ids (reference builds them via `randperm`, so no duplicates within a token)\n29\t- `routing_weights` `[N, K]` bf16 — softmax over the K logits, sums to 1 per token\n30\t- `expert_gate_weights` `[E, H, I]` bf16\n31\t- `expert_up_weights`   `[E, H, I]` bf16\n32\t- `expert_down_weights` `[E, I, H]` bf16\n33\t\n34\tOutput: `output` `[N, H]` bf16.\n35\t\n36\t### Reference algorithm (exact semantics we MUST reproduce)\n37\t\n38\t1. `capacity = max(int((N*K/E) * 1.25), 1)`  (Python `int()` truncates toward zero).\n39\t2. Flatten the `N*K` (token, expert) assignments. `flat_token_ids = arange(N).repeat_interleave(K)`, so the\n40\t   flat index of assignment `(token t, slot k)` is `t*K + k`.\n41\t3. **Stable** sort of the flat assignments by expert id. Stability means within one expert the assignments\n42\t   keep increasing `(t*K + k)` order — i.e. lower token id first, and for the same token lower slot first.\n43\t4. Within-expert position `within_pos = global_sorted_idx - group_start[expert]`.\n44\t5. **Capacity admission:** keep assignments with `within_pos < capacity`. The rest are **dropped** (their\n45\t   contribution to that token's output is simply omitted; there is **no renormalization** of routing weights).\n46\t6. Scatter admitted token rows into a padded batch `expert_inputs[E, capacity, H]` (unfilled rows are zero).\n47\t7. Three batched GEMMs per expert with SwiGLU:\n48\t   - `gate = X_e @ Wg_e`  (`[cap,H]·[H,I]`)\n49\t   - `up   = X_e @ Wu_e`  (`[cap,H]·[H,I]`)\n50\t   - `act  = silu(gate) * up`  — **note:** `gate` and `up` are bf16 GEMM outputs; silu and the multiply are\n51\t     performed in **bf16** (torch rounds each GEMM result to bf16 before the elementwise ops).\n52\t   - `y    = act @ Wd_e`  (`[cap,I]·[I,H]`)\n53\t8. Gather admitted rows back, weight by `routing_weight`, and `index_add_` into `result[N,H]` (bf16\n54\t   accumulation, since `result` is bf16). Dropped tokens contribute nothing; a token that had all 8 experts\n55\t   admitted sums 8 weighted contributions.\n56\t\n57\t**Correctness-critical invariants** we cannot deviate from:\n58\t- Identical **admitted set** (same drop decisions). This depends on the stable-sort order and the exact\n59\t  `capacity` integer. Easiest safe route: reuse the reference's cheap metadata ops in torch (sort / bincount /\n60\t  cumsum / mask) verbatim — these are \"tensor metadata\", explicitly allowed.\n61\t- **No renormalization** of routing weights after dropping.\n62\t- SwiGLU is `silu(gate) * up` with `silu(x)=x*sigmoid(x)`; gate is the first projection, up the second.\n63\t- Weight layouts: gate/up are `[H, I]` (contract over dim-1 = `H`); down is `[I, H]` (contract over `I`).\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## 2. Per-workload characterization (feedback set)\n68\t\n69\t`capacity = int(N*K/E * 1.25)`, `N*K` = total assignments, padded rows processed by the reference = `E*capacity`.\n70\tArithmetic intensity of the three-GEMM core, in FLOP per weight-byte, works out to exactly `≈ capacity`\n71\t(derivation in §3), so the capacity value alone predicts the compute/memory regime.\n72\t\n73\t| uuid (short) | N | capacity | mean load `N*K/E` | assigns `N*K` | padded rows `E*cap` | pad/useful | core FLOP | AI≈cap | regime | atol |\n74\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n75\t| b983758c | 1536 | 96  | 76.8  | 12288 | 15360 | 1.25 | ~1.45 TF | 96  | **memory** | 1.9 |\n76\t| d36f3c8b | 1568 | 98  | 78.4  | 12544 | 15680 | 1.25 | ~1.48 TF | 98  | **memory** | 1.2 |\n77\t| c2a09e88 | 1344 | 84  | 67.2  | 10752 | 13440 | 1.25 | ~1.27 TF | 84  | **memory** | 1.6 |\n78\t| f850f2b7 | 4096 | 256 | 204.8 | 32768 | 40960 | 1.25 | ~3.87 TF | 256 | **compute** | **0.0076** |\n79\t| 2fe16676 | 1571 | 98  | 78.55 | 12568 | 15680 | 1.248| ~1.48 TF | 98  | **memory** | 1.7 |\n80\t\n81\tAll five share `rtol=0.05`, `required_match_ratio=0.98`.\n82\t\n83\tObservations that steer the whole design:\n84\t\n85\t- **Four of five workloads are memory-bound** on weight traffic (capacity ≈ 84–98 < Ampere ridge point ≈ 150,\n86\t  see §3). Only `N=4096` (capacity 256) is compute-bound.\n87\t- **All 160 experts are active in every workload** (mean load 67–205 ≫ 0), so no expert can be skipped and the\n88\t  full weight tensor must be streamed. **Weight traffic is a hard floor** (§3).\n89\t- Padding overhead is a uniform 1.25× because capacity = 1.25× mean load; useful rows ≈ `N*K` minus a small\n90\t  number of dropped tokens on over-subscribed experts.\n91\t- `N=4096` has an unusually tight **`atol=0.0076`** (≈ one bf16 ULP near 1.0). Because `rtol=0.05` also applies\n92\t  and only 98% of elements must pass, large-magnitude outputs are governed by the loose rtol; the tight atol\n93\t  only bites for near-zero outputs. Still, this workload is the one most sensitive to accumulation ordering and\n94\t  drop-set fidelity — treat it as the numerical canary.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 3. Roofline / cost model (A800 sm_80)\n99\t\n100\tA800 ≈ A100 die: bf16 tensor-core peak ≈ **312 TFLOP/s**, HBM2e bandwidth ≈ **~2.0 TB/s**, 108 SMs, 164 KB\n101\tsmem/SM. Ridge point ≈ `312e12 / 2.0e12 ≈ 156` FLOP/byte.\n102\t\n103\t**Weight bytes (streamed once, minimum):**\n104\t`3 tensors × E·H·I × 2 B = 3 × 160 × 6144 × 2560 × 2 ≈ 15.1 GB`. At ~2.0 TB/s the pure weight-streaming floor\n105\tis **≈ 7.5 ms** for *every* workload, independent of N. This is the dominant term and cannot be reduced\n106\t(all experts active, bf16 fixed).\n107\t\n108\t**Core FLOP** = `E·cap · 6·H·I` (gate + up + down = 3 matmuls, 2 FLOP/MAC). `6·H·I ≈ 9.44e7` per row.\n109\t\n110\t**Arithmetic intensity** = `E·cap·6HI / (3·H·I·2) = cap` → **AI (FLOP/byte) ≈ capacity**. Hence:\n111\t- cap ≤ ~156 ⇒ memory-bound ⇒ wall-clock ≈ weight floor ≈ 7.5 ms (workloads 1,2,3,5).\n112\t- cap = 256 (N=4096) ⇒ compute-bound ⇒ core compute ≈ `3.87e12 / 312e12 ≈ 12.4 ms` > 7.5 ms weight floor.\n113\t\n114\t**Where the reference loses time above these floors (the actual optimization budget):**\n115\t1. **Intermediate HBM traffic.** The reference materializes and re-reads padded tensors:\n116\t   `expert_inputs[E,cap,H]` (scatter-write + read twice for gate & up), `gate_out`, `up_out`, `activated`\n117\t   (`[E,cap,I]` each), `expert_outputs[E,cap,H]` (write + gather-read). Rough totals ≈ 1 GB (cap96) to ≈ 3.7 GB\n118\t   (cap256) on top of the 15 GB weights — i.e. ~7% (memory cases) to ~20% (compute case) extra traffic that a\n119\t   fused kernel can largely eliminate.\n120\t2. **Padded-zero compute.** ~20% of the core FLOP is spent on zero-padded rows. Irrelevant when memory-bound;\n121\t   worth ~1.1–1.25× on the compute-bound `N=4096`.\n122\t3. **Launch / small-op overhead.** sort, bincount, cumsum, arange, compare, scatter, 3× bmm, silu, mul, gather,\n123\t   index_add — ~a dozen kernels. On a big GPU with small per-workload data, fixed launch overhead is a\n124\t   non-trivial fraction for the memory-bound cases.\n125\t\n126\t**Honest speedup ceiling.** Weight traffic is fixed, and cuBLAS batched GEMM is already near BW-peak, so the\n127\treachable win is bounded by items (1)–(3): roughly **~1.1–1.3× geomean**, with the largest headroom on the\n128\tcompute-bound `N=4096` (de-padding + fusion) and thin margins on the memory-bound four (intermediate-traffic +\n129\tlaunch-overhead removal only). This realism should temper candidate ambitions and stopping criteria.\n130\t\n131\t---\n132\t\n133\t## 4. Numerical risks\n134\t\n135\t1. **Capacity integer & drop set.** `capacity = int(N*K/E * 1.25)`. Any off-by-one (e.g. computing in fp32 vs\n136\t   float, `round` vs `trunc`) changes which tokens are admitted → wrong rows entirely → can blow past the 2%\n137\t   mismatch budget. Mitigation: compute capacity exactly as the reference and reuse its stable-sort admission\n138\t   metadata in torch.\n139\t2. **Stable-sort order.** Admission keeps the first `capacity` per expert in `(t*K+k)` order. A non-stable sort\n140\t   would admit a different subset on over-subscribed experts. Mitigation: reuse `sort(stable=True)` metadata.\n141\t3. **No routing-weight renormalization.** Dropped assignments are just omitted; remaining weights are NOT\n142\t   rescaled. Must replicate.\n143\t4. **SwiGLU precision ordering.** Reference rounds `gate` and `up` to bf16 *before* silu/multiply. A fused\n144\t   Triton kernel naturally keeps fp32 accumulators and would compute `silu(gate_fp32)*up_fp32` — *more* accurate\n145\t   than the reference. Tolerances (rtol 0.05, 98% match) are generous, so higher precision is expected to be\n146\t   fine, but this is a deviation to watch, especially on the tight-atol `N=4096`. If a candidate fails the\n147\t   canary, an option is to round gate/up to bf16 before the elementwise step to mimic the reference exactly.\n148\t5. **Accumulation dtype in the down-projection & aggregation.** Reference's `index_add_` accumulates in bf16\n149\t   (result is bf16). A Triton scatter that accumulates in fp32 and casts once at the end is more accurate; again\n150\t   generally safe under the tolerances, but a source of small per-element deltas. Atomic-add should target an\n151\t   **fp32** result buffer (bf16 atomics are unreliable on sm_80), cast to bf16 at the very end.\n152\t6. **tl.dot accumulation.** Must use fp32 accumulators for all three GEMMs (Triton default). bf16-accumulate\n153\t   would lose precision on the `K=H=6144` reduction and likely fail the tight-atol canary.\n154\t7. **Empty / min-capacity edge cases.** `capacity` floored at 1. Not triggered by the feedback set (min cap 84),\n155\t   but the kernel must not divide-by-zero or emit NaN for zero-token blocks; masked loads/stores must yield 0.\n156\t8. **bf16 overflow / NaN.** Inputs are unit-scale (randn, Xavier-scaled weights). `H=6144` reductions in fp32\n157\t   are safe; no overflow expected. silu is bounded below by ~-0.278, fine.\n158\t9. **Determinism vs the evaluator's reference.** The evaluator presumably regenerates inputs from `get_inputs`\n159\t   with a fixed seed and compares to the reference `run`. Both our kernel and the reference see identical inputs,\n160\t   so only algorithmic/precision deltas matter, not RNG.\n161\t\n162\t---\n163\t\n164\t## 5. Triton design space\n165\t\n166\tAll options keep weight traffic at the 15 GB floor (unavoidable). They differ in intermediate traffic, padded\n167\tcompute, and complexity. Ampere-specific tooling available: `tl.dot` (mma.sync m16n8k16 bf16→fp32), `cp.async`\n168\tsoftware pipelining via `num_stages`, masked loads for ragged/padded tiles. Blackwell/Hopper features\n169\t(tcgen05/TMEM/TMA/WGMMA/NVFP4) are **out of scope on sm_80** — the KernelWiki corpus is Blackwell/Hopper-first,\n170\tso only its *algorithmic* grouped-MoE patterns (token sorting, block-aligned grouping, fused SwiGLU, scatter-add\n171\taggregation — the vLLM/SGLang `fused_moe` family) transfer; none of its hardware-intrinsic techniques do.\n172\t\n173\t### Option A — Padded-batch grouped GEMM, two kernels (closest to reference layout)\n174\tKeep the reference's `expert_inputs[E,cap,H]` layout; replace the 3 bmm + silu + mul with:\n175\t- Kernel 1: per `(expert, M-block, N-block)` compute gate & up, fuse `silu(gate)*up`, write `h[E,cap,I]`.\n176\t- Kernel 2: per `(expert, M-block, N-block)` compute `h@Wd`, scale by routing weight, scatter-add to result.\n177\t\n178\tPros: simplest; drops `gate_out/up_out/activated` intermediates (fuses silu). Cons: still materializes\n179\t`expert_inputs` and computes all `E*cap` padded rows. Modest win, mostly on memory cases via intermediate\n180\tremoval. Good **first, low-risk candidate** to establish a correct baseline.\n181\t\n182\t### Option B — Sorted-token grouped GEMM with on-the-fly gather (vLLM `fused_moe` style)\n183\tReuse admission metadata to get admitted `(token_id, expert, weight)` already grouped by expert. Build a\n184\tblock-aligned index list (`moe_align`-style: per expert pad the last tile to `BLOCK_M` with a sentinel row).\n185\t- Kernel 1: each program = one `BLOCK_M` tile of one expert. Gather `X` rows via `token_id` (no\n186\t  `expert_inputs` materialization), compute gate & up over full `K=H`, fuse SwiGLU, write `h` to a compact\n187\t  intermediate `[total_aligned_rows, I]`.\n188\t- Kernel 2: load `h` tile, `h@Wd`, multiply by per-row routing weight, `atomic_add` into fp32 result via\n189\t  `token_id`; cast to bf16 at the end.\n190\t\n191\tPros: eliminates `expert_inputs` scatter + reads; compute only ~`sum ceil(count_e/BLOCK_M)*BLOCK_M` rows.\n192\tCons: per-expert counts (67–205) are comparable to `BLOCK_M`, so block rounding reclaims padding only with\n193\tsmall `BLOCK_M` (e.g. 32 → ~cap; 16 → ~15% fewer rows) at some tensor-core-efficiency cost. Atomic-add\n194\tcontention on the `[N,H]` result. Most upside on the compute-bound `N=4096`. **Primary target design.**\n195\t\n196\t### Option C — Single fused megakernel (gate+up+silu+down in one launch)\n197\tInfeasible: the down projection needs the full `I=2560` intermediate per token block; holding `BLOCK_M×I`\n198\tfp32 in smem (e.g. 64×2560×4 = 640 KB) exceeds the 164 KB/SM budget. Would require tiny `BLOCK_M`, killing\n199\tGEMM efficiency. **Rejected.**\n200\t\n201\t### Option D — Split-K / stream-K on the `K=H=6144` reduction\n202\tWith `E=160` experts × several N-tiles the grid already exceeds 108 SMs, so occupancy is fine without split-K;\n203\tsplit-K adds a reduction pass and extra traffic. Keep in reserve only if profiling shows tail/idle SMs on the\n204\tsmallest workload. **Deferred.**\n205\t\n206\t### Cross-cutting knobs (autotune space)\n207\t- `BLOCK_M ∈ {16, 32, 64}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64, 128, 256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32, 64}`.\n208\t- `num_warps ∈ {4, 8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2, 3, 4}` (cp.async pipelining to hide the weight-streaming latency —\n209\t  the single most important knob for the memory-bound majority).\n210\t- Grid ordering / L2 grouping of tiles by expert to maximize weight-tile L2 reuse across the N-tiles of one\n211\t  expert (weights are the reused operand; group programs of the same expert together).\n212\t- fp32 accumulators everywhere; optional bf16-rounding of gate/up before SwiGLU as a numeric-match fallback.\n213\t\n214\t**Recommended progression:** A (correct baseline, low risk) → B with a modest `BLOCK_M` (fusion + de-padding)\n215\t→ autotune `num_stages`/block shapes → consider bf16-round fallback only if the `N=4096` canary fails.\n216\t\n217\t---\n218\t\n219\t## 6. Validation strategy\n220\t\n221\tConstraints: I may **not** run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, the external evaluator directly, or any alternate\n222\tcorrectness harness. The **only** correctness signal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <cid>`, which\n223\truns all five fixed workloads and counts as **one** evaluation against the 100-eval budget. So local \"unit\n224\ttesting\" is not available; correctness must be engineered up-front and confirmed via the sanctioned evaluator.\n225\t\n226\tApproach:\n227\t1. **Reuse reference metadata verbatim** (capacity, stable sort, within-pos, capacity mask, admitted\n228\t   token/expert/weight lists) in torch — this makes the drop set provably identical and removes the biggest\n229\t   correctness risk. Only the three GEMMs + SwiGLU + weighted scatter-add move into Triton.\n230\t2. **Static self-checks inside `run`** (asserts on shapes/dtypes/contiguity, expert-id bounds) so a malformed\n231\t   launch fails loudly rather than silently mismatching.\n232\t3. **Reason about tolerances** per §4: fp32 accumulation should meet the loose rtol on large outputs; the tight\n233\t   `atol=0.0076` only affects near-zero outputs on `N=4096`, cushioned by the 98% match ratio.\n234\t4. **Candidate discipline / budget economy.** Because each eval costs budget and there's no cheap local check:\n235\t   - `c001` = the lowest-risk correct design (Option A style, minimal fusion) to lock in correctness and get a\n236\t     real baseline speedup number and per-workload pass/fail.\n237\t   - Only after `c001` passes all five, iterate toward Option B and autotuning, changing one thing per candidate\n238\t     so each result is attributable.\n239\t   - Treat `N=4096` (tight atol) as the canary; if a change breaks only it, suspect SwiGLU/accumulation ordering\n240\t     and apply the bf16-round fallback rather than reverting the whole design.\n241\t5. **Per-candidate record** (in `candidates.jsonl`): parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation, five\n242\t   per-workload results (pass + speedup), geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage.\n243\t6. **Stopping.** Given the ~1.1–1.3× ceiling in §3, stop when the geomean improvement across a couple of\n244\t   candidates is within noise of the weight-streaming floor, and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason.\n245\t   Never run `final` without explicit operator approval.\n246\t\n247\t---\n248\t\n249\t## 7. Skill usage & environment notes\n250\t\n251\t- **KernelWiki** (invoked): confirmed Blackwell/Hopper-first scope. Its hardware-intrinsic techniques\n252\t  (tcgen05, TMEM, TMA, WGMMA, NVFP4) do **not** apply to A800/sm_80; only its algorithmic grouped-MoE patterns\n253\t  (token sort → block-aligned grouping → fused SwiGLU → scatter-add, i.e. the `fused_moe` family) transfer. The\n254\t  wiki's query scripts require shell execution, which the current sandbox denies, so I relied on the loaded\n255\t  skill description plus first-principles analysis rather than page-level citations.\n256\t- **ncu-report-skill:** profiling is disallowed for this task (no direct profiler runs), so it is not used;\n257\t  bottleneck reasoning is done analytically via the roofline in §3.\n258\t- **Sandbox note:** arbitrary Bash (including a Python calculator and the KernelWiki query scripts) is denied in\n259\t  this environment. All numbers in §2–§3 are hand-derived from the fixed constants and stated for the plan phase\n260\t  to build against; they involve no execution of the operation.\n261\t\n262\t---\n263\t\n264\t## 8. Summary of decisions carried into the plan\n265\t\n266\t- Move only the 3 GEMMs + SwiGLU + weighted scatter-add into Triton; keep admission metadata in torch to\n267\t  guarantee an identical drop set.\n268\t- Two-kernel grouped GEMM (gate+up+SwiGLU → down+weighted scatter-add), fp32 accumulators throughout, fp32\n269\t  result buffer with a final bf16 cast.\n270\t- First candidate: correctness-first (Option A, padded layout, fused SwiGLU). Then evolve to sorted-token\n271\t  Option B and autotune `num_stages`/block shapes for the memory-bound majority.\n272\t- Numeric canary = `N=4096` (atol 0.0076); bf16-round-before-SwiGLU is the reserved fallback if it fails.\n273\t- Expectation: realistic geomean ceiling ~1.1–1.3×; weight streaming (~15 GB, ~7.5 ms floor) dominates and is\n274\t  irreducible.\n275\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84708b82-1793-4117-b276-8f509c6ef623","uuid":"c2fe2ba1-fd75-4e08-ad4f-f3238540e9d3","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:54:37.026Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-012_moe_expert_batched_execution_with_capacity_factor/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/012 MoE Expert Batched Execution with Capacity Factor\n\nRun ID: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-012_moe_expert_batched_execution_with_capacity_factor`\nTarget: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere). Primary implementation: **Triton**. PyTorch allowed only for\ntensor metadata / launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n\nThis document is analysis only. No plan, no solution code is written yet (per workflow steps 2–4).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation semantics (from `task/definition.json`)\n\nCapacity-based, drop-if-overflow MoE feed-forward with SwiGLU experts.\n\nConstants (all fixed by the task axes):\n\n| symbol | meaning | value |\n|---|---|---|\n| `H` | hidden_size | 6144 |\n| `I` | moe_intermediate_size | 2560 |\n| `E` | num_experts | 160 |\n| `K` | num_experts_per_tok | 8 |\n| `cf` | capacity factor | 1.25 (fixed) |\n| `dtype` | all tensors | bfloat16 (indices int64) |\n\nInputs:\n- `hidden_states` `[N, H]` bf16\n- `selected_experts` `[N, K]` int64 — each row is `K` **unique** expert ids (reference builds them via `randperm`, so no duplicates within a token)\n- `routing_weights` `[N, K]` bf16 — softmax over the K logits, sums to 1 per token\n- `expert_gate_weights` `[E, H, I]` bf16\n- `expert_up_weights`   `[E, H, I]` bf16\n- `expert_down_weights` `[E, I, H]` bf16\n\nOutput: `output` `[N, H]` bf16.\n\n### Reference algorithm (exact semantics we MUST reproduce)\n\n1. `capacity = max(int((N*K/E) * 1.25), 1)`  (Python `int()` truncates toward zero).\n2. Flatten the `N*K` (token, expert) assignments. `flat_token_ids = arange(N).repeat_interleave(K)`, so the\n   flat index of assignment `(token t, slot k)` is `t*K + k`.\n3. **Stable** sort of the flat assignments by expert id. Stability means within one expert the assignments\n   keep increasing `(t*K + k)` order — i.e. lower token id first, and for the same token lower slot first.\n4. Within-expert position `within_pos = global_sorted_idx - group_start[expert]`.\n5. **Capacity admission:** keep assignments with `within_pos < capacity`. The rest are **dropped** (their\n   contribution to that token's output is simply omitted; there is **no renormalization** of routing weights).\n6. Scatter admitted token rows into a padded batch `expert_inputs[E, capacity, H]` (unfilled rows are zero).\n7. Three batched GEMMs per expert with SwiGLU:\n   - `gate = X_e @ Wg_e`  (`[cap,H]·[H,I]`)\n   - `up   = X_e @ Wu_e`  (`[cap,H]·[H,I]`)\n   - `act  = silu(gate) * up`  — **note:** `gate` and `up` are bf16 GEMM outputs; silu and the multiply are\n     performed in **bf16** (torch rounds each GEMM result to bf16 before the elementwise ops).\n   - `y    = act @ Wd_e`  (`[cap,I]·[I,H]`)\n8. Gather admitted rows back, weight by `routing_weight`, and `index_add_` into `result[N,H]` (bf16\n   accumulation, since `result` is bf16). Dropped tokens contribute nothing; a token that had all 8 experts\n   admitted sums 8 weighted contributions.\n\n**Correctness-critical invariants** we cannot deviate from:\n- Identical **admitted set** (same drop decisions). This depends on the stable-sort order and the exact\n  `capacity` integer. Easiest safe route: reuse the reference's cheap metadata ops in torch (sort / bincount /\n  cumsum / mask) verbatim — these are \"tensor metadata\", explicitly allowed.\n- **No renormalization** of routing weights after dropping.\n- SwiGLU is `silu(gate) * up` with `silu(x)=x*sigmoid(x)`; gate is the first projection, up the second.\n- Weight layouts: gate/up are `[H, I]` (contract over dim-1 = `H`); down is `[I, H]` (contract over `I`).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Per-workload characterization (feedback set)\n\n`capacity = int(N*K/E * 1.25)`, `N*K` = total assignments, padded rows processed by the reference = `E*capacity`.\nArithmetic intensity of the three-GEMM core, in FLOP per weight-byte, works out to exactly `≈ capacity`\n(derivation in §3), so the capacity value alone predicts the compute/memory regime.\n\n| uuid (short) | N | capacity | mean load `N*K/E` | assigns `N*K` | padded rows `E*cap` | pad/useful | core FLOP | AI≈cap | regime | atol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| b983758c | 1536 | 96  | 76.8  | 12288 | 15360 | 1.25 | ~1.45 TF | 96  | **memory** | 1.9 |\n| d36f3c8b | 1568 | 98  | 78.4  | 12544 | 15680 | 1.25 | ~1.48 TF | 98  | **memory** | 1.2 |\n| c2a09e88 | 1344 | 84  | 67.2  | 10752 | 13440 | 1.25 | ~1.27 TF | 84  | **memory** | 1.6 |\n| f850f2b7 | 4096 | 256 | 204.8 | 32768 | 40960 | 1.25 | ~3.87 TF | 256 | **compute** | **0.0076** |\n| 2fe16676 | 1571 | 98  | 78.55 | 12568 | 15680 | 1.248| ~1.48 TF | 98  | **memory** | 1.7 |\n\nAll five share `rtol=0.05`, `required_match_ratio=0.98`.\n\nObservations that steer the whole design:\n\n- **Four of five workloads are memory-bound** on weight traffic (capacity ≈ 84–98 < Ampere ridge point ≈ 150,\n  see §3). Only `N=4096` (capacity 256) is compute-bound.\n- **All 160 experts are active in every workload** (mean load 67–205 ≫ 0), so no expert can be skipped and the\n  full weight tensor must be streamed. **Weight traffic is a hard floor** (§3).\n- Padding overhead is a uniform 1.25× because capacity = 1.25× mean load; useful rows ≈ `N*K` minus a small\n  number of dropped tokens on over-subscribed experts.\n- `N=4096` has an unusually tight **`atol=0.0076`** (≈ one bf16 ULP near 1.0). Because `rtol=0.05` also applies\n  and only 98% of elements must pass, large-magnitude outputs are governed by the loose rtol; the tight atol\n  only bites for near-zero outputs. Still, this workload is the one most sensitive to accumulation ordering and\n  drop-set fidelity — treat it as the numerical canary.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Roofline / cost model (A800 sm_80)\n\nA800 ≈ A100 die: bf16 tensor-core peak ≈ **312 TFLOP/s**, HBM2e bandwidth ≈ **~2.0 TB/s**, 108 SMs, 164 KB\nsmem/SM. Ridge point ≈ `312e12 / 2.0e12 ≈ 156` FLOP/byte.\n\n**Weight bytes (streamed once, minimum):**\n`3 tensors × E·H·I × 2 B = 3 × 160 × 6144 × 2560 × 2 ≈ 15.1 GB`. At ~2.0 TB/s the pure weight-streaming floor\nis **≈ 7.5 ms** for *every* workload, independent of N. This is the dominant term and cannot be reduced\n(all experts active, bf16 fixed).\n\n**Core FLOP** = `E·cap · 6·H·I` (gate + up + down = 3 matmuls, 2 FLOP/MAC). `6·H·I ≈ 9.44e7` per row.\n\n**Arithmetic intensity** = `E·cap·6HI / (3·H·I·2) = cap` → **AI (FLOP/byte) ≈ capacity**. Hence:\n- cap ≤ ~156 ⇒ memory-bound ⇒ wall-clock ≈ weight floor ≈ 7.5 ms (workloads 1,2,3,5).\n- cap = 256 (N=4096) ⇒ compute-bound ⇒ core compute ≈ `3.87e12 / 312e12 ≈ 12.4 ms` > 7.5 ms weight floor.\n\n**Where the reference loses time above these floors (the actual optimization budget):**\n1. **Intermediate HBM traffic.** The reference materializes and re-reads padded tensors:\n   `expert_inputs[E,cap,H]` (scatter-write + read twice for gate & up), `gate_out`, `up_out`, `activated`\n   (`[E,cap,I]` each), `expert_outputs[E,cap,H]` (write + gather-read). Rough totals ≈ 1 GB (cap96) to ≈ 3.7 GB\n   (cap256) on top of the 15 GB weights — i.e. ~7% (memory cases) to ~20% (compute case) extra traffic that a\n   fused kernel can largely eliminate.\n2. **Padded-zero compute.** ~20% of the core FLOP is spent on zero-padded rows. Irrelevant when memory-bound;\n   worth ~1.1–1.25× on the compute-bound `N=4096`.\n3. **Launch / small-op overhead.** sort, bincount, cumsum, arange, compare, scatter, 3× bmm, silu, mul, gather,\n   index_add — ~a dozen kernels. On a big GPU with small per-workload data, fixed launch overhead is a\n   non-trivial fraction for the memory-bound cases.\n\n**Honest speedup ceiling.** Weight traffic is fixed, and cuBLAS batched GEMM is already near BW-peak, so the\nreachable win is bounded by items (1)–(3): roughly **~1.1–1.3× geomean**, with the largest headroom on the\ncompute-bound `N=4096` (de-padding + fusion) and thin margins on the memory-bound four (intermediate-traffic +\nlaunch-overhead removal only). This realism should temper candidate ambitions and stopping criteria.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical risks\n\n1. **Capacity integer & drop set.** `capacity = int(N*K/E * 1.25)`. Any off-by-one (e.g. computing in fp32 vs\n   float, `round` vs `trunc`) changes which tokens are admitted → wrong rows entirely → can blow past the 2%\n   mismatch budget. Mitigation: compute capacity exactly as the reference and reuse its stable-sort admission\n   metadata in torch.\n2. **Stable-sort order.** Admission keeps the first `capacity` per expert in `(t*K+k)` order. A non-stable sort\n   would admit a different subset on over-subscribed experts. Mitigation: reuse `sort(stable=True)` metadata.\n3. **No routing-weight renormalization.** Dropped assignments are just omitted; remaining weights are NOT\n   rescaled. Must replicate.\n4. **SwiGLU precision ordering.** Reference rounds `gate` and `up` to bf16 *before* silu/multiply. A fused\n   Triton kernel naturally keeps fp32 accumulators and would compute `silu(gate_fp32)*up_fp32` — *more* accurate\n   than the reference. Tolerances (rtol 0.05, 98% match) are generous, so higher precision is expected to be\n   fine, but this is a deviation to watch, especially on the tight-atol `N=4096`. If a candidate fails the\n   canary, an option is to round gate/up to bf16 before the elementwise step to mimic the reference exactly.\n5. **Accumulation dtype in the down-projection & aggregation.** Reference's `index_add_` accumulates in bf16\n   (result is bf16). A Triton scatter that accumulates in fp32 and casts once at the end is more accurate; again\n   generally safe under the tolerances, but a source of small per-element deltas. Atomic-add should target an\n   **fp32** result buffer (bf16 atomics are unreliable on sm_80), cast to bf16 at the very end.\n6. **tl.dot accumulation.** Must use fp32 accumulators for all three GEMMs (Triton default). bf16-accumulate\n   would lose precision on the `K=H=6144` reduction and likely fail the tight-atol canary.\n7. **Empty / min-capacity edge cases.** `capacity` floored at 1. Not triggered by the feedback set (min cap 84),\n   but the kernel must not divide-by-zero or emit NaN for zero-token blocks; masked loads/stores must yield 0.\n8. **bf16 overflow / NaN.** Inputs are unit-scale (randn, Xavier-scaled weights). `H=6144` reductions in fp32\n   are safe; no overflow expected. silu is bounded below by ~-0.278, fine.\n9. **Determinism vs the evaluator's reference.** The evaluator presumably regenerates inputs from `get_inputs`\n   with a fixed seed and compares to the reference `run`. Both our kernel and the reference see identical inputs,\n   so only algorithmic/precision deltas matter, not RNG.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Triton design space\n\nAll options keep weight traffic at the 15 GB floor (unavoidable). They differ in intermediate traffic, padded\ncompute, and complexity. Ampere-specific tooling available: `tl.dot` (mma.sync m16n8k16 bf16→fp32), `cp.async`\nsoftware pipelining via `num_stages`, masked loads for ragged/padded tiles. Blackwell/Hopper features\n(tcgen05/TMEM/TMA/WGMMA/NVFP4) are **out of scope on sm_80** — the KernelWiki corpus is Blackwell/Hopper-first,\nso only its *algorithmic* grouped-MoE patterns (token sorting, block-aligned grouping, fused SwiGLU, scatter-add\naggregation — the vLLM/SGLang `fused_moe` family) transfer; none of its hardware-intrinsic techniques do.\n\n### Option A — Padded-batch grouped GEMM, two kernels (closest to reference layout)\nKeep the reference's `expert_inputs[E,cap,H]` layout; replace the 3 bmm + silu + mul with:\n- Kernel 1: per `(expert, M-block, N-block)` compute gate & up, fuse `silu(gate)*up`, write `h[E,cap,I]`.\n- Kernel 2: per `(expert, M-block, N-block)` compute `h@Wd`, scale by routing weight, scatter-add to result.\n\nPros: simplest; drops `gate_out/up_out/activated` intermediates (fuses silu). Cons: still materializes\n`expert_inputs` and computes all `E*cap` padded rows. Modest win, mostly on memory cases via intermediate\nremoval. Good **first, low-risk candidate** to establish a correct baseline.\n\n### Option B — Sorted-token grouped GEMM with on-the-fly gather (vLLM `fused_moe` style)\nReuse admission metadata to get admitted `(token_id, expert, weight)` already grouped by expert. Build a\nblock-aligned index list (`moe_align`-style: per expert pad the last tile to `BLOCK_M` with a sentinel row).\n- Kernel 1: each program = one `BLOCK_M` tile of one expert. Gather `X` rows via `token_id` (no\n  `expert_inputs` materialization), compute gate & up over full `K=H`, fuse SwiGLU, write `h` to a compact\n  intermediate `[total_aligned_rows, I]`.\n- Kernel 2: load `h` tile, `h@Wd`, multiply by per-row routing weight, `atomic_add` into fp32 result via\n  `token_id`; cast to bf16 at the end.\n\nPros: eliminates `expert_inputs` scatter + reads; compute only ~`sum ceil(count_e/BLOCK_M)*BLOCK_M` rows.\nCons: per-expert counts (67–205) are comparable to `BLOCK_M`, so block rounding reclaims padding only with\nsmall `BLOCK_M` (e.g. 32 → ~cap; 16 → ~15% fewer rows) at some tensor-core-efficiency cost. Atomic-add\ncontention on the `[N,H]` result. Most upside on the compute-bound `N=4096`. **Primary target design.**\n\n### Option C — Single fused megakernel (gate+up+silu+down in one launch)\nInfeasible: the down projection needs the full `I=2560` intermediate per token block; holding `BLOCK_M×I`\nfp32 in smem (e.g. 64×2560×4 = 640 KB) exceeds the 164 KB/SM budget. Would require tiny `BLOCK_M`, killing\nGEMM efficiency. **Rejected.**\n\n### Option D — Split-K / stream-K on the `K=H=6144` reduction\nWith `E=160` experts × several N-tiles the grid already exceeds 108 SMs, so occupancy is fine without split-K;\nsplit-K adds a reduction pass and extra traffic. Keep in reserve only if profiling shows tail/idle SMs on the\nsmallest workload. **Deferred.**\n\n### Cross-cutting knobs (autotune space)\n- `BLOCK_M ∈ {16, 32, 64}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64, 128, 256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32, 64}`.\n- `num_warps ∈ {4, 8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2, 3, 4}` (cp.async pipelining to hide the weight-streaming latency —\n  the single most important knob for the memory-bound majority).\n- Grid ordering / L2 grouping of tiles by expert to maximize weight-tile L2 reuse across the N-tiles of one\n  expert (weights are the reused operand; group programs of the same expert together).\n- fp32 accumulators everywhere; optional bf16-rounding of gate/up before SwiGLU as a numeric-match fallback.\n\n**Recommended progression:** A (correct baseline, low risk) → B with a modest `BLOCK_M` (fusion + de-padding)\n→ autotune `num_stages`/block shapes → consider bf16-round fallback only if the `N=4096` canary fails.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation strategy\n\nConstraints: I may **not** run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, the external evaluator directly, or any alternate\ncorrectness harness. The **only** correctness signal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <cid>`, which\nruns all five fixed workloads and counts as **one** evaluation against the 100-eval budget. So local \"unit\ntesting\" is not available; correctness must be engineered up-front and confirmed via the sanctioned evaluator.\n\nApproach:\n1. **Reuse reference metadata verbatim** (capacity, stable sort, within-pos, capacity mask, admitted\n   token/expert/weight lists) in torch — this makes the drop set provably identical and removes the biggest\n   correctness risk. Only the three GEMMs + SwiGLU + weighted scatter-add move into Triton.\n2. **Static self-checks inside `run`** (asserts on shapes/dtypes/contiguity, expert-id bounds) so a malformed\n   launch fails loudly rather than silently mismatching.\n3. **Reason about tolerances** per §4: fp32 accumulation should meet the loose rtol on large outputs; the tight\n   `atol=0.0076` only affects near-zero outputs on `N=4096`, cushioned by the 98% match ratio.\n4. **Candidate discipline / budget economy.** Because each eval costs budget and there's no cheap local check:\n   - `c001` = the lowest-risk correct design (Option A style, minimal fusion) to lock in correctness and get a\n     real baseline speedup number and per-workload pass/fail.\n   - Only after `c001` passes all five, iterate toward Option B and autotuning, changing one thing per candidate\n     so each result is attributable.\n   - Treat `N=4096` (tight atol) as the canary; if a change breaks only it, suspect SwiGLU/accumulation ordering\n     and apply the bf16-round fallback rather than reverting the whole design.\n5. **Per-candidate record** (in `candidates.jsonl`): parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation, five\n   per-workload results (pass + speedup), geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage.\n6. **Stopping.** Given the ~1.1–1.3× ceiling in §3, stop when the geomean improvement across a couple of\n   candidates is within noise of the weight-streaming floor, and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason.\n   Never run `final` without explicit operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Skill usage & environment notes\n\n- **KernelWiki** (invoked): confirmed Blackwell/Hopper-first scope. Its hardware-intrinsic techniques\n  (tcgen05, TMEM, TMA, WGMMA, NVFP4) do **not** apply to A800/sm_80; only its algorithmic grouped-MoE patterns\n  (token sort → block-aligned grouping → fused SwiGLU → scatter-add, i.e. the `fused_moe` family) transfer. The\n  wiki's query scripts require shell execution, which the current sandbox denies, so I relied on the loaded\n  skill description plus first-principles analysis rather than page-level citations.\n- **ncu-report-skill:** profiling is disallowed for this task (no direct profiler runs), so it is not used;\n  bottleneck reasoning is done analytically via the roofline in §3.\n- **Sandbox note:** arbitrary Bash (including a Python calculator and the KernelWiki query scripts) is denied in\n  this environment. All numbers in §2–§3 are hand-derived from the fixed constants and stated for the plan phase\n  to build against; they involve no execution of the operation.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Summary of decisions carried into the plan\n\n- Move only the 3 GEMMs + SwiGLU + weighted scatter-add into Triton; keep admission metadata in torch to\n  guarantee an identical drop set.\n- Two-kernel grouped GEMM (gate+up+SwiGLU → down+weighted scatter-add), fp32 accumulators throughout, fp32\n  result buffer with a final bf16 cast.\n- First candidate: correctness-first (Option A, padded layout, fused SwiGLU). Then evolve to sorted-token\n  Option B and autotune `num_stages`/block shapes for the memory-bound majority.\n- Numeric canary = `N=4096` (atol 0.0076); bf16-round-before-SwiGLU is the reserved fallback if it fails.\n- Expectation: realistic geomean ceiling ~1.1–1.3×; weight streaming (~15 GB, ~7.5 ms floor) dominates and is\n  irreducible.\n","numLines":275,"startLine":1,"totalLines":275}}}
